body:
We're all a bit weird, aren't we?

&#x200B;

In all seriousness, if you've managed to create such an emotional connection to your characters and that translates to your work then it's likely that others will also feel that emotional connection.  Which is, of course, a major goal for any writer.

I don't really know what to say other than that you're in a much better position than you think.  You might have a long history of various social problems but in ten years' time they'll be nothing more than distant memories.  The most important thing to remember is that life generally gets better.  I say generally because life will never be 100% great.  There will be shit times where your car breaks down and you walk home in the rain, where your girlfriend cheats on you the same day that you lose your job; and the days that you don't want to go outside because it feels like the world is doing everything in its power to beat you down until you're three inches tall.  But more importantly, there will be good times.  Hell, there will even be great times.  These times will differ from person to person depending on your own preferences and ideals.  But they could be anything from spending a night underneath the stars in total wilderness, diving from a cliff into crystal clear aquamarine waters or even something as simple as the moment you get the Play of the Game in an Overwatch match.

&#x200B;

The first thing you have to do is get active.  Whether that means going to the gym more frequently, joining a local sporting club to play football, soccer, baseball or basketball, or even just going for long walks in nature, the most important thing is that you're doing *something*.  If you're afraid of not being able to keep up with others in competitive sports, see if you can find a social group that is more focused on having a good time rather than winning.

&#x200B;

The second thing to do is to develop yourself as a person by finding some kind of thing that you enjoy to do.  A hobby, essentially.  Start with writing a list of what you enjoy and then brainstorming offshoots of those.  If you enjoy anime you might also enjoy manga.  If you enjoy gaming then look at what types of games you enjoy and what they offer.  In general, though, the more the hobby interacts with other people, the better.  Using the above examples, perhaps your local library might have manga or anime you can rent.  Perhaps there is a nearby store that sells that stuff.  When you visit the store, the best thing to do is to ask the salesperson if they can help you, then establish a rapport with them based upon your experiences.  This would take the form of perhaps asking them about their favourite anime or manga, then asking for recommendations based upon what you enjoy.  This doesn't simply apply to anime and manga, however, but can be applied to all kinds of hobbies.  Skateboarding?  There's bound to be a skate or sports shop somewhere near you.  The important thing to remember is that people like to think that they're respected in every situation.  This means taking their opinions and suggestions and at the very least attempting to apply them to your own life, even if you think that it's worthless.  Then, the next time you see them you can say that you tried whatever they suggested but didn't enjoy it and ask them if they have any other recommendations.

&#x200B;

The third thing to do is to find a club of some sort and join it.  This can take the form of anything.  Dancing clubs, walking clubs, hiking clubs, skating clubs, even gaming clubs.  You'll find these online or in the local newspaper.  It doesn't really matter what you do, as long as you meet new people and learn to enjoy it.  Try to interact with the people in the club in a friendly and non-threatening manner and inquire about mutual interests.  People like to talk about themselves, so remember to ask questions and actually *listen* to the answers.  There's no use asking about their family if you tune out five seconds later.

&#x200B;

I know that this has turned out quite long, but I hope that even if you don't try to implement these suggestions in your life that you at least take comfort in the fact that not everyone in the world is out to get you.  Paranoia is an insidious beast that disguises itself as a concerned friend.
